If an approximable map sends the bottom element of the domain of an endomap f₀ to the bottom element of the domain of an endomap f₁, and intertwines f₀ with f₁, then it sends the least fixed point of f₀ to the least fixed point of f₁.

By induction on the index, rewriting the approximants as iterates, the map sends the n-th approximant of f₀ to the n-th approximant of f₁: the zero case is the hypothesis on bottom, and the successor case uses intertwining and the inductive hypothesis. In one direction the least fixed point of f₀ is the directed union of its approximants, the map preserves that union, and each image is an approximant of f₁, hence lies below the least fixed point of f₁. In the other direction, intertwining and the fixed-point equation show that the image of the least fixed point of f₀ is a fixed point of f₁, so minimality places the least fixed point of f₁ below it.
